add("Thermodynamics (Laws & Heat Engines)", "M", "In an adiabatic expansion of an ideal gas, the work done is derived at the expense of its:", ["Potential energy", "Internal energy", "Enthalpy", "Entropy"], 1, "In adiabatic process dQ = 0. First Law: dW = -dU. Work is done by expending internal energy, causing temperature to drop.")
add("Thermodynamics (Laws & Heat Engines)", "H", "An ideal monoatomic gas (γ = 5/3) is compressed adiabatically to 1/8th of its initial volume. The ratio of final pressure to initial pressure is:", ["8", "16", "32", "64"], 2, "P2 / P1 = (V1 / V2)^γ = (8)^(5/3) = (2³)^(5/3) = 2^5 = 32.")
add("Thermodynamics (Laws & Heat Engines)", "H", "Molar heat capacity of a gas at constant pressure C_p is related to C_v by Mayer's relation:", ["C_p + C_v = R", "C_p - C_v = R", "C_p / C_v = R", "C_v - C_p = R"], 1, "Mayer's relation for an ideal gas: C_p - C_v = R.")
add("Thermodynamics (Laws & Heat Engines)", "M", "First law of thermodynamics dQ = dU + dW is a statement of conservation of:", ["Mass", "Energy", "Momentum", "Charge"], 1, "First law of thermodynamics is a restatement of the universal Law of Conservation of Energy.")
add("Thermodynamics (Laws & Heat Engines)", "H", "A refrigerator has coefficient of performance β = 5. If it extracts 200 J of heat from the cold reservoir per cycle, work input required is:", ["40 J", "50 J", "100 J", "1000 J"], 0, "β = Q_cold / W => W = Q_cold / β = 200 / 5 = 40 J.")
add("Thermodynamics (Laws & Heat Engines)", "H", "During an isochoric process, work done by the gas is:", ["Positive", "Negative", "Zero", "Infinite"], 2, "Isochoric process means volume remains constant (ΔV = 0). Work W = P ΔV = 0.")
add("Thermodynamics (Laws & Heat Engines)", "M", "The efficiency of a Carnot engine working between boiling point of water (100°C) and freezing point of water (0°C) is:", ["26.8%", "36.8%", "100%", "0%"], 0, "η = 1 - 273/373 = (373 - 273)/373 = 100 / 373 ≈ 0.268 = 26.8%.")
add("Thermodynamics (Laws & Heat Engines)", "H", "Slope of an adiabatic curve on a P-V diagram is how many times the slope of an isothermal curve at the same point?", ["γ times", "1/γ times", "γ - 1 times", "Identical"], 0, "Isothermal slope dP/dV = -P/V. Adiabatic slope dP/dV = -γ P/V. Adiabatic slope is γ times steeper.")

